WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.080
The runtime for It: Chapter Two has been revealed,
and it's a doozy.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:10.360
Director Andy Muschietti confirmed to Digital
Spy that the It sequel clocks in at 2 hours

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.920
and 45 minutes.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.540
A runtime that epic may send some moviegoers
with small bladders and restless legs running

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:21.060
for the hills, but Muschietti has promised
that the lucky few who have already seen It:

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.680
Chapter Two in test screenings haven't had
a single complaint about how long the film

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:25.500
is.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.320
The director explained that the lengthy runtime
is simply the amount of time the story needed

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:34.950
in the long-awaited It follow-up, and the
extra 30 or so minutes in comparison to the

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.960
original were totally necessary.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.160
Any other length would have meant dragging
the story out or clipping it too short.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:47.800
"For 27 years, I dreamt of you."

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.720
Muschietti explained further:

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:54.280
"A movie is very different when you're writing
the script and you're building a story compared

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:55.960
to what the final product is.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:00.020
At the beginning, when you're writing and
building the beats of the story, everything

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.250
that you put in there seems very essential
to the story.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:08.249
However, when you have the movie finally edited
and it's four hours long, you realize that

00:01:08.249 --> 00:01:12.509
some of the events and some of the beats can
be easily lifted but the essence of the story

00:01:12.509 --> 00:01:13.840
remains intact.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.880
You cannot deliver a four-hour movie because
people will start to feel uncomfortable, no

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.340
matter what they see, but we ended up having
a movie that is two hours and 45 minutes,

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.160
and the pacing is very good."

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:30.160
For those iron-bladdered viewers who actually
would love to see a four-hour version of It:

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.610
Chapter Two, you may not get to experience
such a thing in cinemas, but you'll surely

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.649
be able to watch it from the comfort of your
own home.

00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:42.929
It: Chapter Two producer Barbara Muschietti
noted that there will most likely be a lengthier

00:01:42.929 --> 00:01:47.530
director's cut of the film, presumably included
in a version of the film's home release.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:48.530
She said:

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:52.520
"We will put out a director's cut because
this time it definitely merits it.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.829
We have some amazing scenes that didn't make
it into the movie.

00:01:55.829 --> 00:02:00.259
You have to make choices sometimes and some
things cannot be in this theatrical release

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.400
but are definitely worthy of people seeing
them at a later date."

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.520
But fear not, fright fans.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.340
All the super-scary scenes are featured in
It: Chapter Two, not left for an alternate

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:11.080
cut.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:15.500
Director Muschietti promised that, quote,
"...everything that is too scary is in the

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:19.450
movie," which we can assume are all those
horrifying scenes featuring a smarter, more

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.610
manipulative Pennywise the Dancing Clown,
and the sequence that features thousands of

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:27.920
gallons of fake blood to make what has been
reported to be the bloodiest scene in film

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:28.920
history.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.120
The movie is also confirmed to include two
controversial storylines from the book: a

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:39.450
hate crime that wasn't included in the 1990
miniseries adaptation, and a domestic abuse

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.900
plot that was.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:45.860
165 minutes is a pretty hefty runtime, but
it makes sense in context.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:50.900
It: Chapter Two has a lot of ground to cover,
picking up 27 years after the events of the

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.980
first film to follow the grown-up Losers'
Club.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.760
Considering it has to cover a huge swath of
Stephen King's more than 1,000-page novel,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.980
it's kind of a wonder the filmmakers got away
with a runtime that came in at under three

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:04.720
hours.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:10.140
And, for context, It: Chapter Two clocks in
at 17 minutes shorter than Avengers: Endgame.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:14.350
Fans flocked to the theaters in droves to
see that film, which has gone on to become

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:19.860
the best-earning movie in history, and largely
found no fault with its substantial length.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:25.680
It stands to reason that if the biggest superhero
movie of 2019 was super-sized and still performed

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.350
that well at the box office, then the biggest
horror movie of 2019 should have no trouble

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:33.140
raking in big bucks regardless of its runtime.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.220
"Can you idiots have taken any longer?"

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:36.220
"Shut up, Richie!"

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:37.440
"Yeah, shut up, Richie!"

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.380
It: Chapter Two floats into theaters September
6.

